  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The fire inspector continues his strict check and rejects several fire extinguishers. The restaurant owner, Dmitry, arrives and demands to know the real reason behind this sudden and aggressive inspection. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где ваш огнетушитель?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Он висел здесь в прошлый раз.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы что, смеётесь надо мной?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этот не подходит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вот нормальный огнетушитель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А где его пломба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Добрый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Можно узнать одну вещь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Почему у вас такой интерес к ресторану?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы боремся с плохим руководством.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Был звонок моему начальнику.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Из соседнего ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Всё понятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что, до завтра.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где ваш огнетушитель?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is your fire extinguisher?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'where' with 'fire extinguisher'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Он висел здесь в прошлый раз.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It was hanging here last time.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'he' with 'time'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы что, смеётесь надо мной?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you laughing at me?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'you' with 'me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этот не подходит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This one is not suitable.' Notice how 'this' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'is coming up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вот нормальный огнетушитель.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is a normal fire extinguisher.' Vika links 'there' directly with 'decent' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А где его пломба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And where is its seal?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'and' directly with 'seal'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Добрый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good afternoon.' Dmitry combines 'good' with 'day' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Можно узнать одну вещь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'May I know one thing?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'thing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Почему у вас такой интерес к ресторану?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why do you have such an interest in the restaurant?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'why' and builds toward 'restaurant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы боремся с плохим руководством.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are fighting with bad management.' the inspector links 'we' directly with 'to fight' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Был звонок моему начальнику.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There was a call to my boss.' the inspector combines 'was' with 'boss' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Из соседнего ресторана.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'From the neighboring restaurant.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'neighboring' straight to 'restaurant'. Dmitry answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Всё понятно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is clear.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'everything' with 'clear'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что, до завтра.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well then, until tomorrow.' Notice how 'oh' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'tomorrow'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где ваш огнетушитель?",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Он висел здесь в прошлый раз.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы что, смеётесь надо мной?",
      "gap": 0.5
